Based on the provided context, the evidence does not show regularization of the fine-tuning process hurting target-domain performance. The paper states that a combination of auxiliary penalty terms "produces the most robust model that both preserves performance in the source domain and achieves optimal performance in the target domain" [1]. It also introduces auxiliary penalty terms to regularize fine-tuning and observes the best performance when a combination is used [2]. These auxiliary penalty terms are favored because they do not require storing source-domain data samples [9].

For target-domain results specifically, the paper notes that the hyper-parameters for different auxiliary penalty terms were "intentionally tuned... to generate similar performance on target domains" [10]. This indicates that the compared variants were configured to perform similarly on the target domain, rather than one regularized variant degrading target performance. The context does not report a comparison in which regularization lowers target-domain performance.
